\title{Spectral Bounds on Non-Homogeneous Quadratic Forms over Lattices\\[2mm]
\large Torsion gaps, characteristic vectors, and the covering weight enumerator}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{7 August 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Let $Q(x) = x^{\T} B x$ be a positive definite quadratic form with rational coefficients on the
lattice $L = \Z^n \subset \Q^n$, and let $F(x) = Q(x-t)$ be the non-homogeneous form attached to a
rational shift $t$. The integral solutions of $F(x) = c$ are the lattice points on the $Q$-ellipsoid
of squared radius $c$ centred at $t$. We study the \emph{minimal lattice energy}
$\lamone = \min_{0 \neq m \in L} Q(m)$ and the \emph{spectral gap}
$\mu(t) = \min_{m \in L} Q(t-m)$, the exact threshold below which $F(x) = c$ has no integral
solution. We prove: (i) for every $r$-torsion shift $t$ (i.e. $rt \in L$, $t \notin L$) one has
$\mu(t) \ge \lamone/r^2$, with a rigidity converse identifying the equality case as
$t \equiv w/r \pmod L$ for $w$ realising $\lamone$, a second gap above the extremal classes, and
full $\mathrm{GL}_n(\Z)$-invariance; (ii) completing the square turns this into an unsolvability
criterion for $Q + \ell + c = 0$ that improves the classical archimedean criterion by exactly
$\lamone/r^2$; (iii) a vector $v$ is a \emph{characteristic vector} of a symmetric integral form
if and only if $Q(v + 2u) \equiv Q(v) \pmod 8$ for all $u \in L$, whence the value spectrum of
$Q(x - v/2)$ lies in $Q(v)/4 + 2\Z$ and distinct attained values differ by at least $2$; the
classical fact that a sum of $n$ odd squares is $\equiv n \pmod 8$ is the instance
$v = (1,\dots,1)$; and (iv) for a positive diagonal form $Q(x) = \sum a_i x_i^2$ the spectral gap
at a $2$-torsion class is the weighted Hamming weight $\tfrac14\sum_{i \in s} a_i$, so the
$2$-torsion gap spectrum runs from the packing invariant $\lamone/4$ to the covering invariant
$\tfrac14\sum_i a_i$; for $\Z^n$ it is exactly $\{k/4 : 1 \le k \le n\}$. On multiplicities, every
shifted theta coefficient is even when $2t \in L$, $t \notin L$; the converse is false
($t = (\tfrac12,\tfrac13)$ in $\Z^2$) and is replaced by the exact criterion that, for a positive
diagonal form, all coefficients are even iff some single coordinate $t_i$ is half-integral.
\end{abstract}

